\section{Layout constants}

The header occupies row~0 and the footer the last row, leaving the
content area in between.  We name those offsets so the draw chunks
read as layout statements rather than arithmetic.
[[SHORT_HASH_LEN]] follows Git's own default for short hash
abbreviation, which lets readers recognise hashes at a glance
without losing uniqueness on lab-sized repositories.

<<viewer constants>>=
SHORT_HASH_LEN = 7
HEADER_ROW = 0
CONTENT_START_ROW = 1
FOOTER_OFFSET_FROM_BOTTOM = 2
@


\section{The [[PlaybackViewer]] class}

<<viewer classes>>=
class PlaybackViewer:
    """Interactive curses-based commit viewer.

    Displays commits one at a time with vi-style navigation:
    ``H``/``L`` for previous/next commit, ``j``/``k`` for
    scrolling within a commit, ``q`` to quit.
    """

    def __init__(self, repo, commits, start_index=0,
                 filter_internal=True, trailers=None,
                 tag_map=None):
        self.repo = repo
        self.commits = commits
        self.index = start_index
        self.scroll = 0
        self.filter_internal = filter_internal
        self.trailers = trailers
        self.tag_map = tag_map
        self._detail_cache = {}

    <<player methods>>
@

The trailer map (\cref{sec:run-join}) is the same data for every
commit, so we read it once and keep it.
We read it on first use rather than in the constructor: the viewer
redraws on every keystroke, so re-reading per redraw would make
navigation crawl on a large repository, while doing it in the
constructor would make merely \emph{creating} a viewer touch the
repository.

<<player methods>>=
def _get_trailers(self):
    """Return the trailer map, reading it on first use."""
    if self.trailers is None:
        self.trailers = get_run_trailers(self.repo)
    return self.trailers
@


\subsection{What counts as looking at a commit}
\label{sec:viewer-view-trace}

Playback is itself a recorded run (\cref{sec:review-runs}), and what
makes that record worth keeping is the trace of which commits the
student actually looked at.
The viewer contributes to it by reporting each commit it puts on
screen to [[learnlog.record_view]].

The unit of the trace is the commit that ends up \emph{drawn}, not the
key that produced it, and the two part company often.
[[j]] and [[k]] scroll within the commit already showing; [[L]] at the
last commit and [[g]] at the first one move nothing at all.
A trace built from keystrokes would report six visits to a commit the
student scrolled through once, and a reader cannot tell that apart
from six genuine returns---so the derived measure everyone will want,
\enquote{which run did they keep coming back to}, would be wrong in
exactly the cases it is asked about.
Hence the rule: a movement that leaves the position where it was
records nothing.

Two events are outside the rule for the same reason, that the position
is not what changed.
[[open]] is recorded unconditionally, because the first commit is on
screen through the viewer starting rather than through anything
moving; and a return to a commit seen before \emph{is} recorded again,
because the back-and-forth is the signal, not noise to be
deduplicated.

<<player methods>>=
def _record_view(self, action):
    """Report the commit now on screen to the run recorder."""
    learnlog.record_view(
        self.commits[self.index]["hash"], action,
    )
@

[[record_view]] does nothing when no run is being recorded, so the
viewer never has to ask whether it is being logged---the same bargain
the stream wrappers make with a student's program
(\cref{sec:review-runs}).
That is what lets [[learnlog play -f bundle.learnlog]] from an unwired
interpreter, and a unit test driving [[_handle_key]] directly, share
one unconditional call site with the recorded case.

All movement between commits goes through one method, so that the
\enquote{only when it changed} rule is stated once instead of in each
of the four key branches that could get it wrong independently.

<<player methods>>=
def _go_to(self, index, action):
    """Show commit ``index``, recording it if it changed."""
    moved = index != self.index
    self.index = index
    self.scroll = 0
    if moved:
        self._record_view(action)
@

Resetting [[scroll]] is outside the [[if]] deliberately: pressing
[[G]] while already at the last commit still scrolls back to the top
of it, which is what it always did and what the key means.
Only the trace treats that keypress as a non-event.


\subsection{Running the viewer}

The [[run]] method enters curses mode and drives the main loop.
We use [[curses.wrapper]] to ensure the terminal is restored
even if an exception occurs.

<<player methods>>=
def run(self):
    """Enter curses mode and run the interactive viewer."""
    import curses
    curses.wrapper(self._main_loop)
@


\subsection{The main loop}

Each iteration draws the screen and waits for a keypress.
We redraw on every key because the state may have changed.

We call [[curses.use_default_colors]] so that the viewer inherits
the terminal's native background and foreground colours instead
of forcing white-on-black.
This respects light themes, transparency, and custom colour
schemes---details that matter when learnlog runs on diverse
student and teacher machines.

The [[open]] event is recorded here rather than in [[_draw]], because
[[_draw]] runs again on every scroll keystroke and would turn one
opening into a stream of them.
Recording it before the first draw rather than after also keeps the
trace truthful about a viewer the student quits immediately: the
commit was on screen, so it belongs in the record.

<<player methods>>=
def _main_loop(self, stdscr):
    """Main event loop inside curses."""
    import curses
    curses.use_default_colors()
    curses.curs_set(0)
    stdscr.clear()
    self._record_view("open")

    while True:
        self._draw(stdscr)
        key = stdscr.getch()
        if not self._handle_key(key):
            break
@


\subsection{Drawing the screen}

The screen has three regions: a header showing position and
commit subject, the scrollable content area with the commit
message and diff, and a footer with key bindings.

We build the content as a list of lines, then display the
portion that fits in the terminal window starting from the
scroll offset.
This approach avoids the complexity of [[curses]] pads while
handling arbitrarily long diffs.

We cache the [[git show]] output because fetching it shells out to
Git.
Without caching, every scroll keystroke would re-run the command,
making the viewer sluggish on large repositories.

<<player methods>>=
def _get_detail(self, commit_hash):
    """Fetch and cache the ``git show`` output."""
    if commit_hash not in self._detail_cache:
        self._detail_cache[commit_hash] = \
            get_commit_detail(
                self.repo, commit_hash,
                self.filter_internal,
                self._get_trailers(),
            )
    return self._detail_cache[commit_hash]
@

[[get_commit_detail]] answers [[None]] for a commit whose every run
block was filtered away (\cref{sec:filter-learnlog}), but the viewer
still has to draw something: the commit list it pages through was built
before filtering, and silently drawing the neighbour instead would make
the position counter disagree with the [[H]]/[[L]] keys.
So we draw a placeholder in its place.
Since review runs are shown like any other run, that placeholder is
reserved for genuine plumbing---the [[pip]] install from
[[learnlog init]], and records of other subcommands left by older
versions.

<<player methods>>=
def _draw(self, stdscr):
    """Render the current commit to the screen."""
    stdscr.erase()
    height, width = stdscr.getmaxyx()

    commit = self.commits[self.index]
    detail = self._get_detail(commit["hash"])
    if detail is None:
        detail = "(learnlog internal run)"

    <<draw header>>
    <<build content lines>>
    <<draw content area>>
    <<draw footer>>

    stdscr.refresh()
@

The header shows the position in the commit list, the short
hash, any tags on the run, and the commit subject.
The text is a method of its own so a test can read it without a
terminal, and the tag map is read once, on first use, for the
reason the trailer map is.
We truncate to the terminal width to avoid curses errors.

<<player methods>>=
def _header_text(self, commit):
    """The header line for `commit`, untruncated."""
    if self.tag_map is None:
        self.tag_map = get_tag_map(self.repo)
    names = self.tag_map.get(commit["hash"], [])
    tags = f" (tag: {', '.join(names)})" if names else ""
    return (
        f"[{self.index + 1}/{len(self.commits)}] "
        f"{commit['hash'][:SHORT_HASH_LEN]}{tags} "
        f"\u2014 {commit['subject']}"
    )
<<draw header>>=
header = self._header_text(commit)[:width - 1]
try:
    import curses
    stdscr.addstr(HEADER_ROW, 0, header, curses.A_REVERSE)
    stdscr.addstr(
        HEADER_ROW, len(header),
        " " * (width - len(header) - 1),
        curses.A_REVERSE,
    )
except curses.error:
    pass
@

The content is the reordered [[git show]] output, which places
the diff before the commit message so that code changes appear
first.
When we can compute it, we prepend a compact
[[Time since last run: ...]] line so the pause becomes part of the
scrollable commit narrative rather than competing with the fixed header.

<<build content lines>>=
content_lines = detail.split("\n")
elapsed = format_elapsed(
    elapsed_since_previous_run(self.commits, self.index)
)
if elapsed:
    content_lines = [
        f"Time since last run: {elapsed}",
        "",
        *content_lines,
    ]
@

We display lines starting from the scroll offset, filling
the area between the header (row~0) and the footer
(last row).

<<draw content area>>=
content_start = CONTENT_START_ROW
content_end = height - FOOTER_OFFSET_FROM_BOTTOM
visible = content_end - content_start

self.scroll = max(
    0,
    min(self.scroll,
        len(content_lines) - visible),
)

for i in range(visible):
    line_idx = self.scroll + i
    if line_idx >= len(content_lines):
        break
    line = content_lines[line_idx][:width - 1]
    try:
        stdscr.addstr(content_start + i, 0, line)
    except curses.error:
        pass
@

The footer shows key bindings.
It sits on the last row of the terminal.

<<draw footer>>=
footer = (
    " H/\u2190: prev  L/\u2192: next  "
    "j/\u2193: down  k/\u2191: up  "
    "g: first  G: last  q: quit"
)
footer = footer[:width - 1]
try:
    import curses
    stdscr.addstr(
        height - 1, 0, footer, curses.A_REVERSE
    )
    remaining = width - len(footer) - 1
    if remaining > 0:
        stdscr.addstr(
            height - 1, len(footer),
            " " * remaining,
            curses.A_REVERSE,
        )
except curses.error:
    pass
