\subsection{Handling key presses}

The key handler updates the viewer state and returns [[False]]
when the user presses [[q]] to quit.
We support both the letter keys ([[H]], [[L]], [[j]], [[k]])
and arrow keys for accessibility.

Each branch names the movement it performs and hands the destination
to [[_go_to]] (\cref{sec:viewer-view-trace}), which is what gives the
action words in the trace---[[next]], [[prev]], [[jump-first]],
[[jump-last]]---a single definition each.
The step keys keep their guards rather than clamping through
[[min]]/[[max]], because \enquote{[[H]] at the first commit does
nothing} and \enquote{[[g]] at the first commit returns to the top of
it} are genuinely different behaviours and the guard is what
distinguishes them.
The scroll keys touch no commit and so pass [[_go_to]] by.

<<player methods>>=
def _handle_key(self, key):
    """Process a keypress.  Return ``False`` to quit."""
    import curses

    if key in (ord("q"), ord("Q")):
        return False
    elif key in (ord("h"), ord("H"), curses.KEY_LEFT):
        if self.index > 0:
            self._go_to(self.index - 1, "prev")
    elif key in (ord("l"), ord("L"), curses.KEY_RIGHT):
        if self.index < len(self.commits) - 1:
            self._go_to(self.index + 1, "next")
    elif key in (ord("j"), curses.KEY_DOWN):
        self.scroll += 1
    elif key in (ord("k"), curses.KEY_UP):
        self.scroll = max(0, self.scroll - 1)
    elif key == ord("g"):
        self._go_to(0, "jump-first")
    elif key == ord("G"):
        self._go_to(
            len(self.commits) - 1, "jump-last",
        )

    return True
@

We test the key handler logic without a real curses screen
by calling it directly with key codes:

<<viewer test functions>>=
def test_handle_key_quit():
    from learnlog.viewer import PlaybackViewer
    viewer = PlaybackViewer(None, [{"hash": "a"}], 0)
    assert viewer._handle_key(ord("q")) is False

def test_handle_key_navigation():
    from learnlog.viewer import PlaybackViewer
    commits = [
        {"hash": "a"}, {"hash": "b"}, {"hash": "c"}
    ]
    viewer = PlaybackViewer(None, commits, 0)
    viewer._handle_key(ord("l"))
    assert viewer.index == 1
    viewer._handle_key(ord("l"))
    assert viewer.index == 2
    viewer._handle_key(ord("l"))
    assert viewer.index == 2  # at end, stays
    viewer._handle_key(ord("h"))
    assert viewer.index == 1

def test_handle_key_scroll():
    from learnlog.viewer import PlaybackViewer
    viewer = PlaybackViewer(None, [{"hash": "a"}], 0)
    viewer._handle_key(ord("j"))
    assert viewer.scroll == 1
    viewer._handle_key(ord("k"))
    assert viewer.scroll == 0
    viewer._handle_key(ord("k"))
    assert viewer.scroll == 0  # floor at 0

def test_handle_key_jump_endpoints():
    from learnlog.viewer import PlaybackViewer
    commits = [
        {"hash": "a"}, {"hash": "b"}, {"hash": "c"}
    ]
    viewer = PlaybackViewer(None, commits, 1)
    viewer._handle_key(ord("G"))
    assert viewer.index == 2
    viewer._handle_key(ord("g"))
    assert viewer.index == 0
@

A live run is what turns [[record_view]] from a no-op into an
append, so the tests below supply one: a stand-in [[repo]] object
and an empty trace, both restored afterwards by [[monkeypatch]].
Driving [[_handle_key]] with the real key codes rather than calling
[[_go_to]] directly is the point---the mapping from keys to action
words is exactly what could rot.

The walk visits [[bbb]] three times by three different routes, so the
trace has to keep repeats and has to name each arrival for what it
was:

<<viewer test functions>>=
def test_navigation_records_view_trace(monkeypatch):
    """Each arrival is recorded, revisits included."""
    import learnlog
    from learnlog.viewer import PlaybackViewer
    monkeypatch.setattr(learnlog, "repo", object())
    monkeypatch.setattr(learnlog, "view_trace", [])
    commits = [
        {"hash": "aaa"}, {"hash": "bbb"}, {"hash": "ccc"}
    ]
    viewer = PlaybackViewer(None, commits, 0)

    viewer._record_view("open")
    for key in "llhgG":
        viewer._handle_key(ord(key))
    viewer._handle_key(ord("h"))

    assert [
        (commit, action)
        for _, commit, action in learnlog.view_trace
    ] == [
        ("aaa", "open"),
        ("bbb", "next"),
        ("ccc", "next"),
        ("bbb", "prev"),
        ("aaa", "jump-first"),
        ("ccc", "jump-last"),
        ("bbb", "prev"),
    ]
@

The complement matters just as much, because it is the half that
decides whether the trace measures reading or typing.  Scrolling
inside a commit, and a step or a jump that runs into the end of the
list, all leave the screen showing what it already showed:

<<viewer test functions>>=
def test_a_keypress_that_moves_nothing_records_nothing(
    monkeypatch,
):
    """Scrolling and blocked moves are not views."""
    import learnlog
    from learnlog.viewer import PlaybackViewer
    monkeypatch.setattr(learnlog, "repo", object())
    monkeypatch.setattr(learnlog, "view_trace", [])
    commits = [{"hash": "aaa"}, {"hash": "bbb"}]
    viewer = PlaybackViewer(None, commits, 0)

    for key in "hgjk":
        viewer._handle_key(ord(key))
    assert learnlog.view_trace == []

    viewer._handle_key(ord("G"))
    viewer._handle_key(ord("G"))
    viewer._handle_key(ord("l"))
    assert [
        (commit, action)
        for _, commit, action in learnlog.view_trace
    ] == [("bbb", "jump-last")]
@

